.TH LOG 3 "2026-05-14" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
log, logf, logl, log2, log2f, log2l, log10, log10f, log10l,
log1p, log1pf, log1pl, logp1, log2p1, log10p1 \- logarithmic functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double log(double " x );
.br
.BI "float logf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double logl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double log2(double " x );
.br
.BI "float log2f(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double log2l(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double log10(double " x );
.br
.BI "float log10f(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double log10l(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double log1p(double " x );
.br
.BI "float log1pf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double log1pl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double logp1(double " x );
.br
.BI "double log2p1(double " x );
.br
.BI "double log10p1(double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR log ()
function returns the natural logarithm (base
.IR e )
of
.IR x .
.PP
The
.BR log2 ()
and
.BR log10 ()
functions return the base-2 and base-10 logarithms respectively.
.PP
The
.BR log1p ()
function returns the value of
.BR "log(1 +" " " "x" ),
computed in a way that is accurate even when
.I x
is close to zero \(em the direct
.BI log(1 " + " x )
form would lose precision because
.B 1 + x
rounds out most of the significant bits of a small
.IR x .
.PP
The C23
.BR logp1 ()
function is an alias for
.BR log1p ().
.BR log2p1 ()
and
.BR log10p1 ()
are the base-2 and base-10 forms of the same identity \(em they return
.BR "log2(1 +" " " "x" )
and
.BR "log10(1 +" " " "x" )
respectively, with the same precision guarantees for small
.IR x .
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the natural / base-2 / base-10
logarithm of the argument.
.PP
If
.I x
is NaN, NaN is returned.
.PP
If
.I x
is +1.0 (or 0.0 for the
.BR *p1 / log1p
variants),
.B +0
is returned.
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 (or \-1.0 for the
.BR *p1 / log1p
variants), a pole error occurs:
.B \-HUGE_VAL
is returned and
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
A divide-by-zero floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_DIVBYZERO )
is raised.
.PP
If
.I x
is negative (or less than \-1.0 for the
.BR *p1 / log1p
variants), a domain error occurs: NaN is returned,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM ,
and the invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.
.PP
If
.I x
is +infinity, +infinity is returned (for the
.B *p1
forms,
.B 1 + x
must be evaluated first, so +infinity in
.I x
gives +infinity).
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EDOM
.I x
is negative (or less than \-1.0 for
.BR *p1 / log1p
variants).
.TP
.B ERANGE
.I x
is 0.0 (or \-1.0 for
.BR *p1 / log1p
variants).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Computing the entropy of a probability distribution:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include <math.h>
double entropy(const double *p, int n) {
    double h = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (p[i] > 0.0) h \-= p[i] * log2(p[i]);
    }
    return h;  /* in bits */
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compounding small interest rates accurately:
.PP
.RS
.nf
double effective_rate(double r_per_period, int periods) {
    /* (1 + r)^N - 1, equivalent to expm1(N * log1p(r)) */
    return expm1((double)periods * log1p(r_per_period));
}
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exp (3),
.BR pow (3),
.BR math (7)
.SH STANDARDS
C99, POSIX.1\-2001.  C23 added
.BR logp1 (),
.BR log2p1 (),
and
.BR log10p1 ().
